      "body": "<p>When IT administrators, security officers, and operations managers work with local folders, mapped drives, and external storage containing PDF, DOCX, and XLSX files, the risk is not only losing a file. The real risk is sending a document that looks correct on screen while still carrying internal information the recipient should never see.</p>\n<p>The most common issues come from forgotten legacy files, uncontrolled copies, personal data in unauthorized locations, and missing inventory. These elements are not always visible in a normal viewer, but they can matter in an audit, a dispute, a GDPR review, or a client-facing delivery.</p>\n<p>DocInspector is positioned as a Windows desktop application that processes documents locally. In this scenario, its core value is straightforward: teams can run deep scanning and inventory reporting across hundreds or thousands of files without uploading them to a cloud service and without breaking the original folder structure.</p>\n<p>Practical checklist:</p>\n<ul><li>Run a local audit on the full folder, not only on the files you plan to send.</li><li>Check the main risks: forgotten legacy files, uncontrolled copies, personal data in unauthorized locations, and missing inventory.</li><li>Apply deep scanning and inventory reporting on a working copy, then preserve the original separately.</li><li>Generate an Excel report or audit trail as internal evidence of the workflow.</li><li>Preserve the folder structure so the operational context of the documents is not lost.</li></ul><p>Recommended workflow: create a working copy of the folder, run analysis across the full structure, identify problematic documents, apply the required operations, and export results into a separate output folder. For teams, the final report matters as much as the cleaned file because it documents what was processed and where issues were found.</p>\n<p>For SEO, the article can naturally target phrases such as: sensitive document discovery, PC